WHAT THE PRESCRIBED 18-CELL FORM WOULD HAVE SCORED, and this is the finding
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The design review prescribed 12 accept cells plus SIX declined cells -- tA/tB/tC at mode 0x00f0/
0x04f0/0x08f0, first and repeated.  Intersecting each mutant's reddening set with {the 19 existing
rows} U {those 18 cells}:

    M13, M14, M15, M20, M21, M22   would still have been KILLED  (each reds a tX ycrcb d1 or rpt)
    M23_permit_tiled_d4            *** CLEAN SWEEP *** -- reds ONLY tA/tB/tC ycrcb d4
    M24_permit_d2d8                *** CLEAN SWEEP *** -- reds only d2/d8 cells
    M25_permit_d8                  *** CLEAN SWEEP ***
    M26_permit_d2                  *** CLEAN SWEEP ***
    M27_permit_bc_d2d8             *** CLEAN SWEEP ***

FIVE of the nine enumerated (d) mutants would have been TOTAL ESCAPES of the prescribed form -- the
same class re-entering through the ten cells of the sixteen that the prescription leaves empty, which
is precisely the #499 shape (repair at the address the mutant used, symmetric cell left open) and
precisely what the repair-chain amendment's class-closure rule exists to stop.

AND ONE MORE AGAINST MY OWN INTERMEDIATE PLAN, recorded because it is the same lesson aimed at me:
an earlier cut of this pass drove TEN cells (TLB_A at all four widths, TLB_B and TLB_C at d1 and d4,
LIN_A at d1).  M27_permit_bc_d2d8 reds ONLY (TLB_B,d2), (TLB_B,d8), (TLB_C,d2), (TLB_C,d8) -- exactly
the four cells that plan lacked.  The full 4x4 is what closes it.

EACH ADDED CELL IS INDIVIDUALLY LOAD-BEARING, not padding: M25 reds only the four d8 cells and M26
only the four d2 cells, so neither width is redundant with the other.

